WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.560
DDoS defense begins with knowing what must stay available.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.820
That sounds obvious, but many incidents are made harder by unknown public services,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.040
forgotten domains, unowned APIs, old test systems, undocumented admin panels,

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:20.140
or origins that were meant to sit behind an edge provider but remain directly reachable.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.320
An inventory is not paperwork for its own sake.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.100
It is the map responders use when time is scarce and the service is under pressure.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.520
A useful inventory covers more than public IP addresses.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:38.380
It includes domains, DNS zones, CDN front doors, WAF policies, API gateways,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.280
load balancers, login flows, customer portals, payment paths, status pages,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.620
monitoring endpoints, and third-party integrations.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.880
It also identifies administrative access paths and whether they depend on the same public

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.580
edge as the customer-facing service.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.320
If responders cannot reach dashboards, management interfaces,

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.820
or provider portals during an event, recovery becomes harder.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.600
Dependencies matter because availability is often constrained by the weakest path.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.780
A website may rely on DNS, certificate validation, an identity provider,

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:19.080
a payment service, a queue, a cache, a database, a fraud service, and support tooling.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.860
A DDoS event against one public endpoint can expose hidden coupling in another system.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.280
The defensive inventory should show which dependencies are critical,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.140
which can fail gracefully, which have provider support,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.960
and which have alternate procedures.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.420
Ownership is part of the asset record.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.880
Each critical service should have an owner, an escalation path, a runbook,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.460
provider contacts, and a known business priority.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.360
The team should know which services are supposed to be public, which should be private,

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.660
and which origins should accept traffic only through approved front doors.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.360
Unknown exposure creates incident confusion.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.860
Known exposure can be protected, monitored, tested, and improved before pressure arrives.
